# technology-ai.kb - Technology / AI
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 4 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
ai | definition | Artificial intelligence is software that performs tasks we would normally call intelligent - recognising images, translating, writing, deciding. Modern AI is mostly machine learning: instead of hand-written rules for every case, a model is shown many examples and learns the pattern. A large language model is trained on a very large amount of text to predict what comes next, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state plausible things that are not true. | llm, model, neural | artificial intelligence, machine learning, what is ai, what is artificial intelligence, what is a language model |  |
hallucinate | definition | A large language model predicts the most likely continuation of text, which means it is optimised to sound plausible rather than to be true. When it does not know something, the same mechanism produces a fluent, well-formed and incorrect answer - called a hallucination. It is not a bug that will be patched away; it follows from how these systems are built. Anything that matters should be checked against a real source, and the model should be treated as a useful draft rather than an authority. | hallucination, wrong, accuracy, reliable, trust | can ai be wrong, what is hallucination, is ai accurate |  |
machine learning | definition | Machine learning fits a model to data by adjusting parameters until its output matches the answers, rather than having the rules written by hand. It is split by what is supervised with labelled examples, unsupervised with none, and reinforcement where the system learns from reward. The failure mode to know about is overfitting, where a model memorises its training data and then performs badly on anything new it has not seen. | machine learning, model, training, overfitting, supervised, neural | what is machine learning, what is overfitting, is machine learning the same as ai | machine learning | machine learning
openai | definition | OpenAI builds large language models and sells access to them through an API, with the models being the product and the API the delivery. A language model predicts the next token, which produces broad ability and a real tendency to state things plausibly and incorrectly. That is the honest framing of what these systems are, and it is why verification matters more with them than with a lookup, which cannot invent an answer. | openai, gpt, language model, token, llm, api | what is openai, what is a language model, why do ai models hallucinate | openai | openai
